You're using the term "structure" in a vague manner.  There are plenty
of more random games that have structure -- look at Dungeon Crawl, to
point to just the most recent challenger to Nethack's throne.  There's
a lot that could be learned from that game, although it itself isn't
exactly perfect.  (Its items and monsters don't have nearly as much
personality as Nethack's, it doesn't have a strong ID game, there are
many fewer "special" challenges like stealing from shops, etc).

There is a general perception among roguelike players, though, outside
of Nethack's fanbase, that Nethack is kind of old hat.  One person I
know says it requires learning a database of injokes to play it well.
I don't agree with that -- what Nethack has really done is taken the
raw randomness of Rogue and allowed the player to overcome it with
knowledge.  My claim is that it's possible it's gone too far in that
direction.

