I brought up the wand of wishing only to illustrate how little gold
means to the game.  If the player found a wand of wishing in a shop,
yes, he would be able to get out without paying, every time.  Even if
he's lawful, the alignment penalty is probably negligible.

I consider pet theft to be well-balanced because it takes up so much
time, food and effort.  Credit cloning is a bit more unbalancing; I'd
think Slash'em's solution of not letting animals pick up more than a
few coins at once is probably best for that.

Shops are one of the most awesome things in the game, and have been
extensively balanced.  I get the feeling they would be easy to break.

> There are a lot of things that need to be changed to support players
> actually gathering gold over any significant period of time in order
> to _purchase_ items.  Not that it can't be done, but you get the idea.

(I've been in general brainstorm mode for some time here, as you've
probably noticed....)

One possible solution (maybe not the best) there are to put in more
money sinks, increase prices modestly, and finally make cash a
meaningful influence on score again.  There's lots of money in Nethack
because of its Rogue lineage, where gold was the -only- score, and
when gold slowly decreased in its influence on score the amount in the
game was never scaled back in response to its increased focus as a
trade good, and then when item weights were introduced into the game
it became impossible to carry around all the player could find anyway.

